{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And Israel heard.</b> Scripture relates his humility, that he heard of the defilement of his son's bed and did not command that he be expelled from his house from among his sons and that he not inherit with them, but rather he is counted with them and they are twelve, and he is counted first. Therefore it made of the two paragraph-breaks a single verse, for although it is the beginning of a new matter — coming to count the tribes from the time all of them were born — it hints that Reuben was not rejected because of his deed. And by way of the plain sense it is possible that Reuben disturbed Bilhah's bed out of fear lest she bear [more] to Jacob, for he was the firstborn and he expected to take two portions and would lose more than all the brothers, and he was not afraid of his mother because she was elderly. And perhaps Zilpah had died, or he was concerned for his mother's honor because she was her handmaid, and therefore the birthright was taken from him — measure for measure. And this is the reason for \"and the sons of Jacob were twelve,\" for he did not beget any afterward:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {0 "The paragraph breaks (פרשיות) refer to the petuchot/setumot spacing in the Torah scroll; Ramban addresses why a verse spans two such breaks."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:39:50.225265325Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}